  "referenceCorpus": {
    "_notice": "SYNTHETIC reference passages for retrieval/attribution engineering tests only. Qualitative, non-numeric statements written for this fixture; NOT verified medical guidance and never served as such (sourceType synthetic_test).",
    "sources": [
      {
        "sourceId": "syn-anaemia",
        "title": "Synthetic Test Reference: Haemoglobin",
        "text": "# Haemoglobin overview\nSynthetic test passage. Low haemoglobin can be associated with iron deficiency, blood loss or reduced red cell production. Results are interpreted against the reference range printed by the reporting laboratory.\n\n# Follow-up\nSynthetic test passage. Persistently low haemoglobin is usually followed up with further blood tests such as ferritin and iron studies."
      },
      {
        "sourceId": "syn-glycaemia",
        "title": "Synthetic Test Reference: Glucose control",
        "text": "# Glucose control\nSynthetic test passage. HbA1c reflects average blood glucose over the preceding months. Elevated HbA1c together with elevated fasting glucose is discussed as a marker of hyperglycaemia."
      },
      {
        "sourceId": "syn-glycaemia-monitoring",
        "title": "Synthetic Test Reference: Glucose monitoring",
        "text": "# Monitoring\nSynthetic test passage. Repeat HbA1c measurement is commonly used to monitor glucose control over time, alongside home glucose readings."
      },
      {
        "sourceId": "syn-renal",
        "title": "Synthetic Test Reference: Kidney function",
        "text": "# Kidney function tests\nSynthetic test passage. Serum creatinine and estimated glomerular filtration rate are used together to describe kidney function. A raised creatinine can reflect reduced kidney filtration.\n\n# Potassium\nSynthetic test passage. Raised serum potassium in the setting of reduced kidney function is usually reviewed promptly by a clinician."
      },
      {
        "sourceId": "syn-thyroid",
        "title": "Synthetic Test Reference: Thyroid tests",
        "text": "# Thyroid function\nSynthetic test passage. A suppressed TSH with raised free T4 is discussed as a pattern of an overactive thyroid."
      }
